Carved into the stones of Newgrange five thousand years ago and painted into
the Book of Kells millennia later, the spiral and the interlaced knot run
through Celtic art as a continuous thread, an unbroken line that turns back on
itself without beginning or end.

## The unbroken line

The endless knot has no loose ends. Drawn as a single continuous strand, it
suggests continuity, return, and the absence of a final edge, a quieter cousin
of the centered mandala.

## The quiet mandala of the West

Celtic spirals are less obviously centered than a Tibetan palace or a rose
window, but they share the same instinct: to use pattern and return to point
beyond the everyday toward something whole.

